## Reproduction

1. Arc Pro B70, xe ≥ current, any recent GuC (70.58.0/70.72.1 both affected; 70.72.1 = best).
2. Run sustained LLM inference in Bionic with its GGUF runtime "Vulkan llama.cpp (Linux) 2.73.0" (llama.cpp 2.73.0, Vulkan+AVX2): path Vulkan loader 1.3.204 → Mesa anv (25.2.8) → xe → GuC. Load: ≥20B-param model, ≥40k context, continuous generation 1–2 h (~150k tokens). Actual config: Qwen3.8-27B-Q4_K_M, 60k context.
3. **When it hits:** the freeze is *more likely* at context-management transitions than during steady-state generation (though it can still fault mid-generation): (a) the start of a new chat/turn (the app locks before any generation begins), and (b) the start of a context compaction (a post right before compaction, then it hangs with no progress) — typically around ~150k cumulative tokens. Those transitions are when the driver tears down / rebuilds the GPU context (TLB-invalidation path) — consistent with the Bug A `TD flush timeout` signature.
4. Observe: application freeze (llama-server/main app unresponsive), no kernel output; `cat /proc/<llama>/stack` shows `futex_do_wait` with no xe ioctl; GuC log ring (`guc_log_lfd`) still advancing.


## What we ask from Intel or anyone looking in to this

**Triage the GuC-side stall.** GuC is alive (logging, ~21 ms heartbeat) but under sustained load the host's fences/TLB invalidations/completions are never acknowledged. The periodic ~2 s gaps in the GuC's own log timeline (§Bug B.3) suggest an internal periodic stall — please map the event codes above (70.72.1 source) and inspect what the ~0.21 s motif and the 2 s gaps correspond to.


